### What is the API Gateway pattern?

The API Gateway pattern places a single reverse-proxy service in front of all backend microservices. Clients send every request to this one address, and the gateway routes each request to the correct service while handling cross-cutting concerns like authentication, rate limiting, and aggregation. The client never needs to know the internal topology.

```java
package com.bank.gateway;

import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.route.RouteLocator;
import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.route.builder.RouteLocatorBuilder;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;

@Configuration
public class GatewayRoutes {

    @Bean
    public RouteLocator bankRoutes(RouteLocatorBuilder builder) {
        return builder.routes()
                .route("accounts", r -> r
                        .path("/api/v1/accounts/**")
                        .uri("lb://account-service"))
                .route("payments", r -> r
                        .path("/api/v1/payments/**")
                        .uri("lb://payment-service"))
                .route("cards", r -> r
                        .path("/api/v1/cards/**")
                        .uri("lb://card-service"))
                .build();
    }
}
```

```text
// Client calls: POST /api/v1/payments/transfer
// Gateway routes to: lb://payment-service/api/v1/payments/transfer
// "lb://" means resolve via load balancer + service discovery
```

### How does service discovery support the gateway?

Service Discovery means the gateway resolves `lb://payment-service` through a registry like Eureka to a live instance rather than a hardcoded address. This matters because instances scale up and down dynamically.

```java
package com.bank.gateway;

import org.springframework.cloud.client.discovery.EnableDiscoveryClient;
import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;

@SpringBootApplication
@EnableDiscoveryClient
public class GatewayApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(GatewayApplication.class, args);
    }
}
```

```yaml
# application.yml
spring:
  application:
    name: api-gateway
eureka:
  client:
    service-url:
      defaultZone: http://eureka-server:8761/eureka/
```

```text
// Eureka registry at startup:
// account-service: [192.168.1.10:8081, 192.168.1.11:8081, 192.168.1.12:8081]
// payment-service: [192.168.1.20:8082, 192.168.1.21:8082]
// Gateway resolves "lb://account-service" to one of the three instances
```

### How does the circuit breaker protect synchronous calls?

The Circuit Breaker wraps each downstream synchronous call. When the payment service is unhealthy, the gateway fails fast and returns a fallback instead of hanging and exhausting its own threads.

```java
package com.bank.gateway;

import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.route.RouteLocator;
import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.route.builder.RouteLocatorBuilder;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;

@Configuration
public class ResilientRoutes {

    @Bean
    public RouteLocator resilientBankRoutes(RouteLocatorBuilder builder) {
        return builder.routes()
                .route("payments-resilient", r -> r
                        .path("/api/v1/payments/**")
                        .filters(f -> f
                                .circuitBreaker(cb -> cb
                                        .setName("paymentCircuitBreaker")
                                        .setFallbackUri("forward:/fallback/payments")))
                        .uri("lb://payment-service"))
                .build();
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.gateway;

import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;
import java.util.Map;

@RestController
public class FallbackController {

    @GetMapping("/fallback/payments")
    public Map<String, String> paymentFallback() {
        return Map.of(
                "status", "SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE",
                "message", "Payment service is temporarily unavailable. Please retry.");
    }
}
```

```text
// Normal: Payment Service responds in 5ms → gateway forwards response
// Failure: Payment Service down → circuit OPEN → fallback in <1ms
// Recovery: After 30s → circuit HALF_OPEN → test call → if OK → CLOSED
```

### How does request aggregation work?

A mobile dashboard needs account, payment, and card data. Without a gateway the app would make three round trips. With the gateway, the app makes one call and the gateway fans out to three services and merges the results.

```java
package com.bank.gateway;

import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.PathVariable;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.client.WebClient;
import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.Map;

@RestController
public class DashboardAggregator {

    private final WebClient webClient;

    public DashboardAggregator(WebClient.Builder builder) {
        this.webClient = builder.build();
    }

    @GetMapping("/api/v1/dashboard/{customerId}")
    public Mono<Map<String, Object>> getDashboard(
            @PathVariable String customerId) {

        Mono<Object> account = webClient.get()
                .uri("http://account-service/api/v1/accounts/{id}", customerId)
                .retrieve().bodyToMono(Object.class)
                .timeout(Duration.ofMillis(300))
                .onErrorReturn(Map.of("status", "unavailable"));

        Mono<Object> payments = webClient.get()
                .uri("http://payment-service/api/v1/payments/{id}/recent", customerId)
                .retrieve().bodyToMono(Object.class)
                .timeout(Duration.ofMillis(300))
                .onErrorReturn(Map.of("status", "unavailable"));

        Mono<Object> cards = webClient.get()
                .uri("http://card-service/api/v1/cards/{id}", customerId)
                .retrieve().bodyToMono(Object.class)
                .timeout(Duration.ofMillis(300))
                .onErrorReturn(Map.of("status", "unavailable"));

        return Mono.zip(account, payments, cards)
                .map(tuple -> Map.of(
                        "account", tuple.getT1(),
                        "payments", tuple.getT2(),
                        "cards", tuple.getT3()));
    }
}
```

```text
// Without gateway: 3 round trips from mobile = 3 x 100ms = 300ms
// With gateway: 1 call, 3 parallel backend calls = max(100ms) = ~100ms
// If card service is slow → falls back to "unavailable" → rest still renders
```

### What is the Backend-for-Frontend (BFF) variant?

BFF runs a dedicated gateway per client type. The mobile gateway returns a lean payload optimized for small screens and slow networks. The web gateway returns a richer payload for desktop browsers. Each BFF knows exactly what its client needs.

```java
// Mobile BFF: lean payload
// GET /mobile/api/v1/dashboard/CUST-1001
// Response: {balance: 75000, recentTxnCount: 5}

// Web BFF: rich payload
// GET /web/api/v1/dashboard/CUST-1001
// Response: {balance: 75000, transactions: [...], cards: [...], loans: [...]}
```

### How is the API Gateway different from a service mesh?

The API Gateway handles north-south traffic (external clients to internal services). A service mesh handles east-west traffic (internal service-to-service communication). They are complementary.

| Aspect | API Gateway | Service Mesh (Istio, Linkerd) |
|---|---|---|
| Traffic direction | North-south (external → internal) | East-west (internal ↔ internal) |
| Handles | Routing, auth, rate limiting, aggregation | mTLS, retries, circuit breaking, observability |
| Deployment | One or few gateway instances | Sidecar proxy per service instance |
| Business logic | None (thin edge) | None (infrastructure) |

### What are common mistakes? (wrong vs correct)

The most dangerous mistake is putting business logic in the gateway. The gateway should handle cross-cutting concerns only.

```java
// WRONG: business logic in the gateway
@GetMapping("/api/v1/transfers")
public Mono<TransferResult> transfer(@RequestBody TransferRequest req) {
    // Gateway checking balance, calculating fees, applying rules
    BigDecimal fee = req.amount().multiply(new BigDecimal("0.01"));
    if (fee.compareTo(new BigDecimal("500")) > 0) {
        fee = new BigDecimal("500"); // Business rule in gateway!
    }
    // This turns the gateway into a monolith
}

// CORRECT: gateway only routes, service owns the logic
// Gateway route: /api/v1/transfers → lb://payment-service
// Payment Service handles balance check, fee calculation, business rules
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer keeps the gateway thin and generic. It handles routing, security, aggregation, and resilience, but the actual business rules live in the services. Overloading the gateway with domain logic recreates the coupling microservices were meant to remove. The gateway must also be horizontally scaled because it is a single point of failure by design.

```java
// Senior checklist for a healthy gateway:
// 1. Routes: predicate + URI, no business logic
// 2. Auth: JWT validation at the edge, forwarded as trusted context
// 3. Rate limiting: per-client token bucket backed by Redis
// 4. Circuit breaker: per-route with fallback
// 5. Aggregation: simple view composition, not domain orchestration
// 6. Horizontal scaling: multiple replicas behind a load balancer
```
